# Line endings, pinned deliberately.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. The repo had no .gitattributes, so line endings were decided per-machine by
# core.autocrlf. On a Windows checkout with autocrlf=true, git rewrote every text file to CRLF in the
# working tree while storing LF in the object database. Prettier 3 defaults to endOfLine=lf, so it
# then reported 711 files as "unformatted" on a tree where only TWO genuinely were - the other 709
# differed by line ending alone. Linux CI, reading the same LF objects, saw none of it. That gap made
# a local `prettier --check` permanently red and a CI one permanently green, for the same commit.
#
# Pinning eol=lf cannot change what CI sees: the objects are already LF and CI runs on ubuntu-latest.
# It only makes a Windows working tree agree with the objects, and therefore with CI.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary files must never be eol-normalised. This is not cosmetic: packages/assets/tests/
# bounded-png.test.ts reads the squoosh .wasm as a Buffer and CHECKSUMS it with createHash, so a
# single byte rewritten by normalisation would fail the test with an error that looks nothing like
# its cause. The same applies to the harfbuzz .wasm used by packages/text and tests/integration.
*.wasm  binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
